-- Lean 4: Curves of Bounded Variation and Arc Length in Metric Spaces
import Mathlib.Topology.MetricSpace.Basic

-- Path variation over a finite partition in a metric space
def PathVariation {X : Type*} [PseudoMetricSpace X] (γ : ℝ → X) (pts : List ℝ) : ℝ :=
  match pts with
  | [] =&gt; 0
  | [_] =&gt; 0
  | a :: b :: rest =&gt; dist (γ a) (γ b) + PathVariation γ (b :: rest)

-- A curve is rectifiable if its polygonal variations are bounded above
def IsRectifiable {X : Type*} [PseudoMetricSpace X] (γ : ℝ → X) (a b : ℝ) : Prop :=
  BddAbove { v : ℝ | ∃ pts : List ℝ, pts.Head? = some a ∧ pts.getLast? = some b ∧ v = PathVariation γ pts }

8. Summary & Quick-Reference Cheat Sheet

Mount Any Drive in 3 Steps:
# 1. Scan and find the UUID or Label
lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,FSTYPE,LABEL,UUID,MOUNTPOINTS

# 2. Mount by Label (No sudo required)
udisksctl mount -b /dev/disk/by-label/&lt;LABEL&gt; --options ro

# 3. Safely unmount before pulling the USB cable
udisksctl unmount -b /dev/disk/by-label/&lt;LABEL&gt;


Recovery Toolkit:

Fix Dirty NTFS Drive: sudo ntfsfix -d /dev/disk/by-label/&lt;LABEL&gt;
Force Lazy Unmount (Stuck Device): sudo umount -l /mnt/windows or fusermount -z -u &lt;path&gt;
Check Drive Health (S.M.A.R.T.): sudo smartctl -a /dev/disk/by-id/&lt;DISK_ID&gt;

By combining persistent disk identifiers (/dev/disk/by-label/ and by-uuid/) with modern unprivileged tooling like udisksctl, you can reliably manage hot-plugged storage on any Linux distribution without hardcoded device failures or hibernation lockouts.
